eme court  justices she joined the court last june  after spending eight years as a federal  trial judge in the nation's capital so  president biden has promised to  diversify the federal judiciary in terms  of both demographic background and  professional diversity um and judge  jackson ticks both of those boxes she'll  be the first black woman on the court um  and she also brings background  background as a criminal defender um  which we have not seen um in many many  years i would say  that  my different professional background  than many of the court of appeals judges  including my  district court background which will be  different if i'm confirmed than many of  my my colleagues  would bring value  it's sort of like the oliver wendell  holmes quote that the life of the law is  not logic it's experience  and so i've experienced life  uh in in perhaps a different way than  some of my colleagues because of who i  am  and that might  be valuable uh i hope it would be  valuable if i was confirmed to the court  republicans have tried to pin her down  on this question of the quote-unquote  living constitution the idea that  constitutional interpretation should  adapt the social change should evolve  over time  um she said in 2013 at her confirmation  at a confirmation hearing the district  court that she disagreed with that um  but more recently at her appeals court  confirmation hearing she really just  declined to talk about it and defer to  the supreme court the constitution is an  enduring document it is uh it has  the supreme court has said  a fixed meaning  that we're to look to the original  words in the constitution and  interpret  as lower court judges interpret  provisions the way in which the supreme  court does and they  look at the text and meaning judge  jackson has not said very much about  what kind of a presence she will be on  the court um as is common with lower  court judges we don't know very much  about her judicial philosophy  and when she was a